architecture federation-twin
  canonical_name FederationTwin
  family external
  domain External
  maturity measured
  semantic_question Which externally owned networks does this institution hold as subjects, what grammar do they publish themselves in, and what must that grammar not be read to mean?
  # An external network is foreign state in its own right, and it is the highest
  # density foreign subject there is: holding the federation first means its
  # identity scheme, addressing, traversal, pagination and event vocabulary are
  # learned once, and its members are reached through the grammar it already
  # publishes. The alternative is rediscovering fifty thousand actors
  # independently and storing fifty thousand copies of one protocol's semantics.
  node_types FederationProfile
  node_types FederationTwin
  node_types TraversalContract
  node_types CandidateAddress
  edge_types speaks
  edge_types traverses_to
  edge_types discovered_by
  edge_types peers_with
  state_types observed
  state_types participant
  state_types traversed
  # A federation is a subject AND a carrier, and these are separate roles.
  # Subject: identity, topology, governance, version, reliability. Carrier:
  # discovery, routing, member resolution, events. Collapsing subjecthood into
  # transport capability loses the distinction that makes the twin lawful --
  # GitHub is an institution and a source carrier, not one thing.
  invariant protocol semantics are authored once in a profile and an instance stores only its address, version, extensions, frontier and deltas; this is dictionary compression and it is the reason a federation of fifty thousand instances is tractable
  invariant the negative grammar travels with the protocol, not with each instance that speaks it; what `follows` must not be read to mean is a property of ActivityPub, and an instance that forgot to say so would otherwise be an instance where it was safe to infer
  invariant a member is reached through the federation's own published traversal, never by independent reconstruction; the network has already compressed identity, routing, collections, membership coordinates and continuation
  invariant an observed federation edge carries its source federation, carrier, observation time and evidence, and stays in the foreign graph; only a separately admitted relationship enters the institutional one
  forbidden_inference an observed federation -> a joined one (fetching what a network says about itself is observation, and observation is not membership)
  forbidden_inference a federation member -> an admitted provider
  forbidden_inference a participant row -> reach (publishing on a carrier says the bytes are served, never that anyone fetched them or that a peer accepted them)
  forbidden_inference nodeinfo user counts -> an audience this institution can address
  computational_form observation
  native_format json
  external_binding activitypub
  external_binding mcp
  external_binding a2a
  external_binding beckn
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
  witness cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/tests/federation_test.sh
  known_gap peers are not yet traversed: the frontier records that nodeinfo was read, and a federation-of-federations walk past depth one is declared and unbuilt
  known_gap a source this entry named could not be opened from this checkout -- /var/lib/bonfyre/cms/content-types/federation_profile.json; /var/lib/bonfyre/cms/content-types/federation_twin.json. The line is removed rather than repointed to something that merely looks similar, and what remains listed is the evidence this architecture actually stands on.
architecture foreign-address-identity
  canonical_name ForeignAddressIdentity
  family external
  domain External
  maturity measured
  semantic_question When two references differ, are they two resources or one resource written twice?
  # Resolution compared a requested address to the stored one with strcmp. A
  # reference carrying a tracking parameter, a default port, a fragment, an
  # upper-case host or a missing root slash therefore resolved to nothing: the
  # institution held the resource and answered that it did not, and held the
  # same thing twice whenever both forms were observed.
  node_types CanonicalAddress
  node_types Deformation
  edge_types canonicalizes_to
  state_types closed
  state_types missed
  state_types false_merge
  invariant folding is confined to what a standard or a declared policy permits: scheme and host case, a default port, an empty root path, a fragment, and named tracking parameters
  invariant the path is never folded; paths are case-sensitive and a non-root trailing slash distinguishes two resources a server may serve differently
  invariant a canonical match is a strong heuristic over addressing and never a claim of identity, which is settled by the retained bytes
  forbidden_inference two addresses that canonicalize alike -> the same resource
  forbidden_inference an unfolded scheme change -> two distinct resources (it is undetermined until the digests are compared)
  computational_form observation
  native_format json
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
