architecture external-estate-index
  canonical_name ExternalEstateIndex
  family external
  domain External / Catalogue
  layer surface
  semantic_question What does the outside publish, as distinct from what this estate reads?
  purpose Four thousand two hundred and ninety-four external resources arrived as a compiled index -- public APIs, MCP servers, RSS estates, machine protocols, x402 deployments. The census holds two hundred and sixty-nine rows and has walked sixty-five, and the obvious move was to pour one into the other. That would have broken the census's own law: it is a map of ways in, deliberately small, and most of this index is specifications, repositories and documentation. Fifteen hundred entries are github.com, which are citations rather than sources. So two registers, and the distance between them is the honest statement: the estate knows of four thousand things, has mapped two hundred and sixty-nine as ways in, and has actually read sixty-five.
  maturity implemented
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/external_resource.json
  consumes ForeignTwin
  publishes DerivedView
  internal_calculus each resource keeps the index's own EXT identifier, the source estate it was compiled from, the functional families the cross-index tags it with, its url and any related links. The canonical enumeration is the source-organised half of the document; the functional half is a cross-index where one resource appears under several functions, which is why it holds eight thousand lines for four thousand resources.
  invariant a catalogued resource carries no tier, twin, receipt or last-seen; those belong to a source this estate has actually asked, and a catalogue row wearing them would claim an observation nobody made
  invariant the catalogue does not feed the census automatically; promotion is an authoring act, because a way in is a thing somebody decided to read rather than a thing that exists
  invariant the register is sliceable like every other surface; four thousand rows that can only be listed is a file with extra steps
  invariant the identifier is the index's own, so a re-import updates rather than duplicates and the compilation stays traceable to its source document
  cannot_infer a catalogued resource -> a read one; this says the outside publishes it, never that this estate has asked, been permitted, or holds anything from it
  cannot_infer a functional tag -> a capability; the index says where a resource was filed, not what it will do
  cannot_infer the size of the catalogue -> the reach of this estate; the two registers are far apart on purpose and the distance is the point
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/external-estate.test.mjs holds the two registers apart: the catalogue is an order of magnitude larger than the census, no catalogue row carries an observation field, the census has not swollen to the catalogue's size, descriptions do not repeat their own url, and the register slices and compounds like every other surface
  known_gap nothing links a census row to the resource it came from; the two registers are related only by host, so promotion is by hand and provenance is not recorded
  known_gap the index was compiled elsewhere and imported once; there is no re-import path that reconciles changes, so a later edition would have to be diffed by hand
  known_gap four thousand rows were written one at a time because the store has no bulk path, which took eight minutes of sustained work; a larger index would need one

