  forbidden_inference an unflagged source -> a screened one

architecture foreign-publication-corpus
  canonical_name ForeignPublicationCorpus
  family external
  domain External / Retained Material
  layer external/corpus
  semantic_question What did the observed world actually say, rather than which sources this estate could have asked?
  purpose Promote the items inside retained syndication bodies into publication occurrences, each carrying the twin whose receipt holds it, the digest that identifies it, and the rights basis it was retained under. The estate stops holding a list of feeds and starts holding what those feeds published.
  maturity implemented
  node_types InternetSource
  node_types PublicationOccurrence
  edge_types promotes
  edge_types corroborates
  command BonfyreCMS
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/internet_source.json
  witness cmd/BonfyreCMS/tests/publication_promotion_test.sh runs the native CMS with Bun assertions for strict UTF-8 decoding, latin-1 conversion, entities, idempotency, batch counts and rights/assertion refusals without a Python test interpreter
  invariant an item is identified by source, link and title hashed together, so re-promoting a feed adds nothing and a feed that repeats an item stores it once
  invariant every promoted item carries assertion_state OBSERVED and a rights basis of retained-public-rss-observation; the estate records that a foreign party said this, never that it is true
  invariant a field whose bytes are not valid UTF-8 is transcoded from latin-1 rather than stored raw; text that is already well-formed is untouched
  forbidden_inference a promoted item -> an endorsement, a republication right, or a claim that what the feed said is true
  forbidden_inference corroboration across sources -> truth; independent syndication of one wire story is one source wearing several names
  forbidden_inference this corpus -> currency; every item is as old as the body it was promoted from
  known_gap the promoter existed, was correct, and had never run: `internet_source` held zero rows, so a native path from a retained feed body to publication items sat complete and unused while the estate had 500 conformant feeds on disk. Cataloguing the feeds was mistaken for having their contents.
  known_gap promoting one source per process reloads and validates 54 content-type schemas before reading a single item, so most of the wall time of a 500-feed estate was spent re-reading its own schema directory. A batch form that keeps the loop inside one process was added for that reason.
  known_gap the promoter copied feed bytes into a TEXT column without transcoding them. 100 of the first 6,281 titles were invalid UTF-8 -- a publisher declaring ISO-8859-1 put raw high bytes into the database, SQLite stored them without complaint, and every reader afterwards had to guess. Measured on the corpus before it was fixed.
  known_gap corroboration is counted on normalized title and canonical link, which finds identical syndication and misses the same event reported in different words. Semantic corroboration needs the vector index over this corpus and has not been built.
  known_gap this corpus was built as row-by-row SQLite inserts at roughly six seconds a feed while the estate holds an activated columnar substrate for exactly this shape of work. Daft and Lance are registered at `measured`, Parquet already carries 544MB of observation facts with a digest-and-slice contribution model, and none of it was consulted. The pipeline is correct and the storage choice was made by not looking.
  known_gap Feldera and Hydro are activated substrates for incremental and distributed dataflow, and a 500-feed promotion is the shape of work they exist for. It ran as a serial loop in one process.
  known_gap the promoter refreshed the family index once per row and then, after that was fixed, once per feed -- and an index rebuild costs the whole table, so a 500-feed promotion was quadratic in the corpus it was building. Deferred to one rebuild at the end of a batch, the full estate promotes in 170 seconds where a feed alone had cost six.
  known_gap the corpus is indexed on hashed-token geometry and the retrieval is noise: a tennis seed returns a train crash and a birdhouse, an earthquake seed returns figure skating. Only the identical-title neighbour is genuinely near, which is corroboration rather than retrieval.
  known_gap two organs of this estate disagree about that geometry. `bonfyre-embed` refuses to return a hashed representation when a learned one was asked for -- "hashed-token geometry is not a learned metric and distances are not comparable across the two" -- and the CMS ANN index builds one and serves it. The refusal is right and it is not enforced anywhere but in the organ that makes it.
  known_gap the estate had a working learned embedder the whole time and could not find it: the default model name resolves to a directory that does not exist, while nomic-embed-text-onnx is installed with its vocab and produces 768-dimensional vectors. The corpus was indexed badly because a default pointed at nothing.
  source_path ~/.bonfyre/estate/publications/corpus.db
  source_path ~/.bonfyre/estate/publications/vectors.db
  known_gap the first 22,962-item corpus and its half-built vector store were written to a session scratch directory and were lost with it. The rebuild lives under ~/.bonfyre/estate/publications beside the estate it was promoted from, because a corpus is estate state and not an intermediate result.
  known_gap the learned index answers where the hashed one could not: "tennis championship final" returns the tennis items at cosine distance 0.906-0.959, where the CMS hashed-token index had returned a train crash and a birdhouse for a tennis seed. The CMS ANN index is still hashed and still served; nothing yet routes corpus search through the learned store.
  known_gap the first learned re-embedding was stopped at 3,160 rows and discarded: it was being written by a tokenizer that stored collapsed vectors for non-Latin titles and dropped typographic punctuation into [UNK] for Latin ones. The store is rebuilt from zero by the corrected tokenizer, because one corpus written by two tokenizers is two geometries with one name.
  known_gap about 7 percent of the corpus -- Cyrillic, Arabic, Bengali, Devanagari and CJK headlines -- is invisible to the installed English model and is refused at embedding rather than indexed; those items remain in the corpus and are unreachable by semantic search.
  invariant feed text is XML: entities are decoded once, after identity is computed from the bytes as the feed wrote them, so decoding changes what is stored and never which occurrence an item is
  known_gap the promoter stripped tags and unwrapped CDATA and never decoded an entity, so 1,799 of 22,962 titles (7.8 percent, from 216 feeds) and 1,200 links were stored as markup -- some Cyrillic feeds entirely as hex entities. The embedder then read "quot" and "x43e" as words, and titles sharing an escaped quote embedded as near-duplicates. Rows were repaired in place by `bonfyre-cms publication repair-entities`, keeping all 22,962 occurrence ids distinct, and the repaired titles re-embedded.
  known_gap 6 titles still carry an entity after decoding, because their feeds escaped twice: "&amp;quot;" decodes once to "&quot;". XML decodes once and a second pass would be a guess about the publisher's intent, so they are left as the feed wrote them.
  known_gap the learned store holds 21,221 of 22,962 titles at 768 dimensions. 1,741 are refused as too little seen by the English vocabulary; the last pass pruned 199 rows the tightened rule no longer admits and replaced the vectors of 1,679 entity-repaired titles. Every stored row's text equals its corpus title, and artifacts and vectors agree row for row.
  known_gap semantic corroboration now finds the same event in different words across different feeds -- a Chile nursing-home fire, a Philippine ferry death toll, the BRICS declaration, a Zomato cash-on-delivery fee -- with no differently-worded pair at cosine 1.000 and no pair at or above 0.90 that shares no word. Before the tokenizer, entity and visibility fixes the top of the same list was collapse: headlines matched on "g20", "sargan" and an escaped "quot". 116 cross-feed pairs sit at or above 0.90. Nothing here calibrates a threshold for "the same event", and near neighbours remain candidates for corroboration rather than corroboration.
  known_gap corpus search on the learned store is reachable only through bonfyre-embed and bonfyre-vec on the command line. The CMS ANN index is still hashed-token geometry and is still the one a surface would query.

architecture destination-release
  canonical_name DestinationRelease
  family external
  domain External / Observed material on its way to a destination
  layer contract
  semantic_question A source is ready for a destination. Has anything actually been delivered to it, and if the source moves, which deliveries stop being current?
  purpose The lifecycle between an observed source and a destination that carries its material: eleven declared states of which three are deliveries, five typed change consequences, and a declaration of which fields each destination carries so a change can invalidate only what it touches. Compiled from CUE, computed from records, and reached through bonfyre-project foreign release-compile, release-deliver and release-impact.
  maturity measured
  node_types DestinationRelease
  node_types SourceReleaseDelta
  node_types DestinationCarries
  edge_types delivers_to
  edge_types invalidates
  internal_calculus readiness and delivery are different facts, and the release projection had only the first. A source contract with a declared extractor and retained material read ready_for_extraction, and the one field capable of contradicting it -- public_program_delivery_verified -- was a literal zero printed beside it, so an operator could not tell whether nothing had been delivered or nothing was being counted
  internal_calculus a state is a delivery because the compiled lifecycle says that state delivers, and for no other reason; a caller comparing state strings itself is a caller that will eventually compare the wrong one
  internal_calculus a delivery is its receipt: the digest of the bytes that were materialized, so a receipt cannot name a file whose contents have since changed
  internal_calculus a blocked destination names the binding it lacks, because a blocked state with an empty reason is indistinguishable from a bug
  internal_calculus a change invalidates only a destination declared to carry the field that changed; refreshing on a timer is what happens when nothing knows which destination a change bears on
  internal_calculus staleness is measured against the estate itself rather than against a second stored snapshot: recompose the fragment from the archive as it stands and see whether it still hashes to what the receipt recorded
  invariant compiling admissibility can never produce a delivering state, because it materializes nothing
  invariant a compiler owns only the states it computes: a recompile may move a row between admissible and blocked and may not touch one that has delivered
  invariant a delivery is refused across catalogue generations rather than continued, so a receipt never names bytes that were not in the release it claims
  invariant a failed delivery writes no state and never alters what was observed
  invariant stale is not withdrawn: a staled delivery keeps its receipt and its count, and what was delivered is still there
  invariant the bytes a receipt names exclude when they were written, because a fragment that carries its own timestamp can never hash the same twice
  forbidden_inference a delivered destination -> a reuse licence, the destination agreeing the material is correct, or authority to publish it again after the source moves
  forbidden_inference an admissible destination -> a delivered one, a published one, or permission that survives a change in the source
  forbidden_inference a cosmetic or absent delta -> a source that did not move; a delta is a statement about the fields this estate compared
  forbidden_inference a delivery this closure leaves alone -> a delivery judged current; it is only one unaffected by the deltas examined
  forbidden_inference a typed consequence -> the source's own intent in changing
  source_path schemas/control/destination-release.cue
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/tools/compile_destination_release.zig
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/generated/native_destination_release.h
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/destination_release.json
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/source_release_delta.json
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/tests/destination_release_test.sh
  witness 2026-09-26 eleven states and five consequences compiled from CUE, three states declaring themselves deliveries, by a generator that refuses an unknown field
  witness 2026-09-26 compiled on the estate: 2,442 source contracts across five destinations gave 2,194 admissible and 10,016 blocked, every blocked row naming its binding -- 2,442 shop destinations with no price bound to the source coordinate, 2,439 media and 2,439 rights with no declared extractor, 257 search destinations with nothing retained to index
  witness 2026-09-26 delivered end to end: the search destination for api.wikimedia.org's featured feed materialized 45 retained items into 6,842 bytes whose sha256 matches the receipt recorded against the row, and the release summary's delivery figure moved from a printed 0 to a computed 1
  witness 2026-09-26 six typed deltas over the estate's own 23,954 observed prices, each naming a source identity that carries more than one amount -- usaspending, medicaid/nadac, azure retail, wikidata P2284, openfoodfacts and openrouter -- keyed on the identity prices bind by so they can reach the destinations that carry the field
  witness 2026-09-26 two defects the measurement caught in the work itself. Recompiling admissibility with INSERT OR REPLACE reset the delivered row to admissible and destroyed its receipt reference, so the compiler now owns only admissible and blocked. And the delivered fragment hashed bytes that included its own materialized_at stamp while the drift check recomposed without it, so every delivery read as stale within a second of being made -- a staleness detector that could only say yes; both paths now compose through one canonical composer
  witness 2026-09-26 held by components/BonfyreProject/tests/destination_release_test.sh, which asserts the refusals rather than the happy path: a destination with no materializer refused, a source with nothing retained refused with the missing material named, a wrong generation refused with no row moved, a recompile leaving a delivery intact, a price change not staling a fragment that carries no amount, archive drift staling the destination that does carry it, and a staled row keeping its receipt and count
  known_gap only the search destination has a materializer. shop, spatial, media and rights declare extraction this estate has not built, and delivering one is refused rather than reported as work that did not happen
  known_gap the deltas this estate can compute come from observed prices and from recomposing its own fragments; no source is compared against a retained earlier body, so a boundary or disappearance consequence is declared and never yet observed
  known_gap nothing schedules any of these three commands; an operator runs them, and the adaptive refresh policy the target describes has no measurement behind it yet
  interacts destination-release-reads-the-twin

interaction destination-release-reads-the-twin
  source destination-release
  destination foreign-twin
  relation delivers_from
  preserves the twin as a cached projection that is not remote truth: a delivery arranges material already retained and fetches nothing
  drops any path by which delivering could alter what was observed, or by which a failed delivery could change the source's standing
  forbidden_inference a delivery -> a fresh reading of the source, or verified availability of what it carries
  witness 2026-09-26 the delivered fragment was composed from the item archive with no network call, and a refused delivery left every destination row where it was
  binding components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
  binding schemas/control/destination-release.cue

architecture discovery-set
  canonical_name DiscoverySet
  family external
  domain External / A working set over observed publishers
  layer contract
  semantic_question Which publishers in the ledger meet a stated set of criteria, and which of those has a person actually accepted?
  purpose A set defined by criteria that run against every publisher in the frontier rather than by a list of members. Ten criterion kinds, each bound to the column it reads; four verdicts, two of which a machine may not set. Reached through bonfyre-project discovery run and discovery decide, and served at /s/sets/all and /s/set/<id>.
  maturity measured
  node_types DiscoverySet
  node_types DiscoveryCandidate
  node_types CriterionKind
  edge_types proposes
  edge_types decided_by
  internal_calculus the estate's existing collections are frozen lists: a source_collection names its members in a source_refs array somebody typed, so nothing can say why a publisher is in one and nothing can propose a new one. A working set is the other shape, and the difference is that criteria can be argued with
  internal_calculus a criterion may only read a column declared for it in CUE, so a set cannot express a judgement the estate holds no data for; the evaluator builds SQL from the compiled table and never from a string in C
  internal_calculus the criteria compute what is reachable and stop there. Two of the four declared verdicts may not be set by a machine, so accepting or rejecting is somebody's act with their name and their reason on it
  internal_calculus a bounded page is not a measurement: the number matched is counted over the whole ledger and reported separately from the rows that crossed the carrier
  invariant a run writes only a verdict the contract lets a machine write, and never disturbs a candidate a person has already decided
  invariant a criterion kind the contract does not declare is refused by name rather than ignored, and a set with no criteria is refused rather than matching the whole estate
  invariant a decision carries the name of whoever made it; an unnamed actor is refused
  invariant a publisher no run has proposed cannot be accepted into a set
  invariant at least one declared verdict is beyond a machine's reach, asserted in the build
  forbidden_inference matching every criterion -> semantic relevance, a reading of the publisher, or a judgement about its quality
  forbidden_inference a proposed candidate -> a source this estate has read, asked for, or endorsed
  forbidden_inference membership of a set -> a claim the publishers in it are related in the world
  forbidden_inference an accepted candidate -> permission to fetch, contact or enrol anything, or a claim the publisher knows the set exists
  forbidden_inference a host suffix -> jurisdiction, ownership or sector; .org is not a charity and .edu is not a school
  source_path schemas/control/discovery-set.cue
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/tools/compile_discovery_sets.zig
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/generated/native_discovery_sets.h
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/main.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/discovery_set.json
  source_path cmd/BonfyreCMS/content-types/discovery_candidate.json
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/tests/discovery_set_test.sh
  witness 2026-09-26 ten criterion kinds and four verdicts compiled from CUE by a generator that refuses a criterion reading a column its table does not have, and refuses a verdict set in which every verdict may be set by a machine
  witness 2026-09-26 criteria ran against the real ledger: 29,256 publishers evaluated, and host_suffix=.edu with publishes_plane=calendar proposed seven -- api.artic.edu, calendar.fiu.edu, calendar.tccd.edu, calendar.tcu.edu, calendar.unt.edu, calendar.utdallas.edu and calendar.utk.edu -- using the same plane classifier the institution room counts with
  witness 2026-09-26 a second set, protocol_in=dcat,socrata,ckan,json_api with min_channels=3, matched 7,285 publishers of the 29,256. The first version reported 500, which was the page limit presented as a measurement; the count is now taken over the whole ledger and the page size reported beside it
  witness 2026-09-26 the human line holds in practice: a run wrote seven candidates all pending, and four attempts to decide were refused -- a verdict a run writes, a verdict nobody declared, a publisher no run had proposed, and a decision with no actor named. Accepting calendar.unt.edu and rejecting api.artic.edu were both recorded with the actor and the reason
  witness 2026-09-26 a re-run re-evaluated the set and left both decided candidates exactly as they were, which is the property that makes a set safe to keep running
  witness 2026-09-26 served at /s/set/school-calendars: one accepted, five waiting on a person, one rejected, each criterion shown with the table and column it actually queries, and the closing sentence's count of machine-forbidden verdicts read from the compiled contract rather than written into the prose
  witness 2026-09-26 held by components/BonfyreProject/tests/discovery_set_test.sh, whose fixture separates the two ways a publisher can fail: one has a calendar and the wrong suffix, one has the suffix and only a feed, and both are excluded
  known_gap nothing schedules a run. The monitoring field records what a set is meant to be watched for and no timer reads it, so a set is as current as the last time somebody ran it
  known_gap a criterion reads the ledger and never the source, so a set cannot express anything about what a publisher actually publishes beyond the shape of its addresses
  known_gap there is no way to accept or reject from the page: the room shows what is waiting and the decision is a command-line act, so the operator loop is legible but not yet closed in a browser
  interacts discovery-set-reads-the-frontier

interaction discovery-set-reads-the-frontier
  source discovery-set
  destination source-frontier
  relation filters_without_reading
  preserves the ledger as a record of addresses: a set queries recorded columns and fetches nothing
  drops any path by which proposing a candidate could read, admit, or contact the publisher
  forbidden_inference a candidate proposed from the ledger -> an address anyone has asked for
  witness 2026-09-26 both sets were evaluated entirely against root and channel columns with no network call, and every candidate was written as pending
  binding components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
  binding schemas/control/discovery-set.cue

architecture browser-host
  canonical_name BrowserHost
  family external
  domain External / Beside the page
  layer external
  semantic_question A person is on a page in their own browser. What has this estate already kept from it, and can they have a copy kept without leaving the page?
  purpose A native-messaging host and a side-panel extension. The host is a carrier: a page's address arrives in an envelope and is answered by the same owner-backed tools the MCP surface calls. It owns no store and no operation. The panel says in plain words what has been kept from the page in front of the reader and keeps a copy when they ask.
  maturity measured
  node_types OperationEnvelope
  node_types HostSession
  interacts foreign-twin
  source_path integrations/browser/host/bonfyre-browser-host.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/host/install.sh
  source_path integrations/browser/host/com.bonfyre.browser_host.json.template
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/manifest.json
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/panel.js
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/panel.html
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/background.js
  source_path integrations/mcp/recursive_overlay_tools.mjs
  witness integrations/browser/tests/browser-host.test.mjs speaks the browser's side of the wire to a real host process: replies are framed as Chrome's native messaging defines, an unknown operation and a non-envelope are refused, only a public https address is carried, keeping a page is refused unless the envelope says the person asked, a session ends after 64 operations, an owner's refusal is carried with its reason, a reply over the browser's limit is cut to its receipt, an oversized envelope ends the session, and a page never read answers an empty history from the real BonfyreProject owner without writing a receipt
  witness 2026-10-03 the opt-in case in the same file read https://example.com/ through the host with a confirmed ask and the page's history then showed one kept observation, both answered by BonfyreProject foreign observe and history
  internal_calculus one connection is one session: the panel holds a port for as long as it is open, so related questions about a page do not start a process each
  internal_calculus an envelope carries an operation, an address and whether the person asked; a reply carries a receipt and never a retained body, because the browser refuses a reply over 1 MiB
  internal_calculus a twin is named for the host a page was read from, so one site's pages share one
  invariant the host carries hello, collected, kept, history, observe, capture, hold, read-only provider_admit and the seven account-enrollment operations; every enrollment change needs confirmed: true; kept, history and observe are ForeignTwin's through the tools the MCP surface uses, capture is the page-capture organ's, and hold is the work queue's
  invariant provider_admit validates a bounded native MessagePack admission against the pinned Aurekai origin, actor and destination, exact retained WebWork revision, declared provider and current challenge; it does not authenticate a provider account, collect a secret or complete the work
  invariant provider continuation selects a provider-only side panel; ordinary customer work never inherits operator enrollment lanes or page-retention history
  source_path integrations/browser/provider-contract.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/host/provider-admission.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/provider-continuation.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/provider-content.js
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/panel-bootstrap.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/panel-provider.mjs
  witness integrations/browser/tests/provider-continuation.test.mjs
  witness integrations/browser/tests/provider-continuation.browser.test.mjs
  forbidden_inference a successful installed-extension fixture -> a real provider sign-in, native user-extension activation or a verified production account session
  invariant observe, capture and hold run only when the envelope carries confirmed: true, which the panel sets from the reader's own click
  invariant a held selection is at most 8 KiB and the same selection from the same page is one piece of held work, not two
  invariant the panel reads a selection only after the reader grants that one site, at the moment they ask for the selection
  witness integrations/browser/tests/browser-host.test.mjs holds a selection in the real BonfyreQueue: refused without the reader's ask and nothing written, held once under family held-selection with the page, the text and the note, held again as the same mission, and an empty or oversized selection refused; a capture is kept under the foreign root by twin and the reply carries hashes and sizes, not bodies
  invariant an address that is not public https, or that carries credentials, is refused before any owner is called
  forbidden_inference a connected host -> authority to act for the person browsing
  forbidden_inference a page observed from the panel -> a true statement about what the page describes
  source_path integrations/browser/tests/extension-live.test.mjs
  witness integrations/browser/tests/extension-live.test.mjs loads the unpacked extension into Chrome for Testing with the host registered in a throwaway profile and drives the panel's own script: a page nothing was kept from says so and enables its controls; the reader's click keeps a copy through BonfyreProject and the history shows it with one receipt and one body on disk; a second click keeps the page as rendered with all five representations; a selection is held in the real queue and refused without the reader's ask; an address that is not public is refused in the reader's words with every control disabled; and the panel follows the tab the reader is on (20 expectations, against https://example.com/)
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/workbench.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/observation.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/capture.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/panel-commerce.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/extension/recurring-commerce.mjs
