architecture browser-engine-provider
  canonical_name BrowserEngineProvider
  family external
  domain External / Engines borrowed from the installed browser
  layer external
  semantic_question The browser already on this machine carries a PDF writer, a PDF reader and a GPU stack. Can the estate use them as operations without building any of them?
  purpose Three operations over the installed Chromium, each naming the engine that did the work and what it cost: HTML to PDF through the browser's print path (Skia), PDF to text through its reader (PDFium), and a WGSL compute program on the GPU (Dawn, with Tint compiling the shader). Building those engines natively is a multi-gigabyte project per engine and none is packaged for this machine; borrowing them is a process start. The receipts are what would justify, or not, taking a smaller native dependency later.
  maturity measured
  node_types EngineReceipt
  interacts page-capture
  source_path integrations/browser/engines/chromium-engines.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/capture/chrome-session.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/tests/session-lifecycle.test.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/tests/navigation.test.mjs
  invariant navigation completion matches a fresh event for the requested frame and document loader; same-document navigation waits for its frame and URL event, and a navigation error or download cannot count as a loaded document
  witness integrations/browser/tests/navigation.test.mjs drives a reused Chrome tab through a delayed script, a redirect, repeated fragment navigation, a download failure and an unsafe-port error; the earlier helper returns while the second document is loading and reports the failed navigation as successful
  known_gap the local attachment fixture produced both a navigation ERR_ABORTED and browser connection closure in the installed headless Chrome; both are rejected, but the protocol isDownload response branch was not exercised by this browser
  forbidden_inference a document load event -> application readiness or successful HTTP status; asynchronous application work and response validation belong to the caller
  invariant browser protocol requests clear their timeout on response or failure; closing the connection rejects pending requests and releases their timers before owned process and profile cleanup
  witness integrations/browser/tests/session-lifecycle.test.mjs verifies process exit and profile removal after successful replies, protocol errors, caller errors and browser disconnection with a pending command; the prior helper completes its work but remains alive until the request deadlines
  source_path integrations/browser/tests/chromium-engines.test.mjs
  witness integrations/browser/tests/chromium-engines.test.mjs writes a two-page document with a table to PDF and reads it back: the reader reports two pages and every phrase including the table's cells, and the read names the hash of the file the writer produced; a file that is not a PDF and an address that is not public https are refused
  witness integrations/browser/tests/chromium-engines.test.mjs runs the field's FNV-1a placement hash as a WGSL program over 100,000 values on a non-fallback adapter and matches the reference on every value; a deliberately wrong reference is counted as 128 mismatches of 256, and a shader that does not compile is refused with the compiler's message
  witness 2026-10-03 Chrome 146 on Apple metal-3, same program, zero mismatches at each size. Inside the page: 100,000 values 6.9 ms (execute 1.5, readback 3.8) against 3.2 ms for the reference on the processor; 1,000,000 values 39.3 ms (execute 4.5, readback 32.4) against 22.1 ms; 4,000,000 values 137.4 ms (execute 9.0, readback 123.6) against 83.2 ms. The whole call, including starting the browser and passing the values in and out, took 1.2 s, 3.0 s and 19.2 s
  internal_calculus one headless browser per call, in a throwaway profile with its sandbox on; a document supplied as HTML is served to it from this process on loopback, so rendering reads nothing from the network
  internal_calculus the shared unit of a GPU program is the WGSL, the input, the value kind and the expected output with its tolerance; the same four could be handed to a native Dawn build or to a page
  internal_calculus PDF text is read through the built-in viewer's controller, which is the browser's private interface; when a release moves it the operation says so rather than returning nothing
  invariant every result names its engine, and a GPU result carries compile, upload, execute and readback times separately
  invariant an integer program must match its reference exactly; a float program is compared within a stated tolerance
  forbidden_inference zero mismatches on this adapter -> the same floats on another device
  forbidden_inference a short execute time -> a fast operation (for this hash the processor was faster at every size once upload and readback are counted, and the whole call is dominated by starting a browser)
  forbidden_inference text read from a PDF -> what a person sees on the page; a scanned page has none
  forbidden_inference these engines borrowed -> Skia, PDFium or Dawn available as native libraries; nothing links them, and a machine without a Chromium browser has none of this
  witness integrations/browser/tests/chromium-engines.test.mjs runs a nearest-vector search (2,048 queries against 20,000 sixteen-dimensional vectors, small-integer values so every distance is exact) and gets the reference's index for every query, reading back only the 2,048 answers. 2026-10-03 on Apple metal-3: 1,205 ms for the JavaScript reference on the processor against 28.6 ms GPU execution, 259 ms with the shader compiled
  witness integrations/browser/tests/chromium-engines.test.mjs reads a PDF written by macOS Quartz rather than by the browser, every line intact, and reports a picture-only PDF as one page with no characters and no invented text
  forbidden_inference the nearest-vector figures -> the GPU beating a native implementation; the reference is JavaScript on one core, not vectorised C
  known_gap two GPU programs have been run: a trivial hash, where the processor wins once readback is counted, and a nearest-vector search, where the GPU wins by a wide margin over a JavaScript reference. Neither is an estate workload yet, and no comparison against the estate's own native kernels exists
  known_gap the PDF reader path depends on a private viewer interface (pluginController_, docLength_) verified on Chrome 146 and Chrome for Testing 131; it returns text in file order with no positions, no page boundaries and no images. A picture-only page yields nothing because nothing here recognises text in pictures, and an encrypted PDF has not been tried
  known_gap nothing in the estate calls these yet: no surface prints a record to PDF, PDF intake does not use the reader, and no organ runs a GPU program through it. WebRTC audio processing and Perfetto are not carried by this path

architecture receipt-pack
  canonical_name ReceiptPack
  family external
  domain External / Where observations are kept
  layer external
  semantic_question A hundred thousand observation receipts are a hundred thousand small files. Can they be one store that says exactly what the files said, for a twentieth of the disk?
  purpose The ForeignTwin owner's receipts packed into one SQLite file beside the loose ones: each receipt stored whole under its file name, compressed against a dictionary trained on the receipts themselves and carrying a checksum. The owner reads the pack first and then the loose files, and a loose file with a packed name wins, so an observation written after packing is never hidden. Packing changes where receipts live and not what any of them says.
  maturity measured
  node_types ReceiptPack
  node_types PackVerification
  interacts foreign-twin
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/Makefile
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/tests/receipt_pack_test.sh
  witness components/BonfyreProject/tests/receipt_pack_test.sh packs 301 receipts, one longer than the eight kilobytes a reader looks at: every packed receipt is byte-identical to its file; conflicts, admissible, hydration, history, resolve and twins answer the same from the pack alone as from the directory; a receipt written after packing is seen; a loose file wins over the packed one of its name and verify then fails; and one byte changed inside one packed receipt is refused by verify and reported by a reader
  witness 2026-10-03 the live estate's 135,989 receipts, read and not changed: 251 MB of text occupying 607 MB on disk packed to 29.0 MB in 75 s; all 135,989 identical to their files on verify; conflicts, admissible and hydration byte-identical between the earlier binary on the loose directory, the new binary on the pack alone, and the new binary with both present; those three scans took 23 to 24 s over the loose files and 2 to 3 s over the pack
  internal_calculus a receipt is one or two kilobytes of nearly the same JSON, so a dictionary of up to 112 KB trained on an even spread of them carries most of each one; the dictionary is sized to what it learns from, so a small estate pays for a small one
  internal_calculus as files each receipt costs a filesystem block: 251 MB of text occupied 607 MB. Transparent filesystem compression does not help, because a compressed file still takes its block (607 MB became 542 MB)
  internal_calculus packed rows are held in name order so finding whether a loose file shadows one is a search; with both present that is 136,000 of each
  invariant packing only reads the receipts directory; the pack takes its name only once it is complete
  invariant every packed receipt carries a checksum, and a row that fails it, or comes back at another length, is reported as damage and never read around
  invariant a loose receipt with a packed name is the newer word on that receipt
  forbidden_inference a packed receipt -> a verified observation; the pack moves receipts, it does not check what they claim
  source_path cmd/BonfyreWebAddress/src/bf_receipt_store.h
  source_path scripts/content-screen.mjs
  witness 2026-10-04 the web-address organ reads receipts through one shim at its eight listing and opening sites. Its /foreign/receipts route returned the same 135,989 rows, byte for byte in content, from the earlier binary on loose files, the new binary on a root holding only the pack, and the new binary on loose files; 13 of its 14 receipt and foreign tests pass and the fourteenth fails the same way with the earlier binary
  witness components/BonfyreProject/tests/receipt_pack_test.sh retires loose files against a pack: 300 unchanged files are removed, the one receipt appended to after packing is kept and still read first, the owner's answers do not change, and retiring twice removes nothing
  witness 2026-10-04 the live receipts packed in place and verified (135,989 identical), a restorable archive of every loose file written (18.5 MB), and the loose files retired: 608 MB on disk became 28 MB, with the owner's conflicts and hydration answers and the live site's receipt listing unchanged
  witness 2026-10-04 the retirement was then undone within four minutes. A scheduled publish pass had been running since midnight with its own server started from the binary built before this change; that server listed no receipts once the files were gone. All 135,989 files were restored from the archive in 43 s and that server listed them again. Retirement is to be repeated when that pass has ended
  invariant a loose receipt is removed only while its bytes still equal the packed copy; one appended to since packing is kept
  forbidden_inference every reader in source migrated -> every running process migrated; a process started before the change keeps the code it started with
  forbidden_inference the loose files restored -> that publish pass unaffected; pages it walked in those four minutes that read receipts may have been refused or rendered without them, and its log does not separate them from its usual refusals
  forbidden_inference a clean verify -> the loose files and the pack staying identical; a receipt written later exists only as a loose file until the next pack
  witness 2026-10-04 06:54 UTC, after that publish pass had ended and no server from the earlier binary remained: the pack verified again (135,989 identical) and the loose files were retired. Receipts are 28 MB on disk; the live site lists the same 135,989 rows as before the change and the owner's conflicts answer is unchanged. The archive of the loose files (18.5 MB) is kept
  known_gap the web organ's other four patched sites use the same shim but were not each driven by a request here. Receipts written from now on are loose files until the next pack, and nothing packs on a schedule
  source_path components/BonfyreProject/tools/receipt_table.cpp
  witness components/BonfyreProject/tests/receipt_pack_test.sh packs 42 bodies holding 32 distinct contents: each content is stored once, every name reads back its file's exact bytes including an empty body and random bytes, an absent name is refused, and one changed byte in a packed body is refused on read and on verify
  witness 2026-10-04 the live estate's 3,074 retained bodies, read and not changed: 934 MB packed to 391 MB in 246 s, 2,814 distinct contents, all 3,074 identical to their files on verify in 9 s. An earlier estimate of 280 MB, made from the first 400 files, was wrong: the larger bodies include already-compressed formats
  witness components/BonfyreProject/tests/receipt_pack_test.sh turns the packed receipts into a typed Parquet table (bonfyre-receipt-table) and checks its counts, its column types and that it agrees with the owner about one address
  witness 2026-10-04 the live receipts as a table: 135,847 observations in a 9.1 MB Parquet written in 1.1 s; the owner's conflicts question asked of it through DuckDB answered in 54 ms against 3 s from the pack and 23 s from loose files, agreeing with the owner on 12,623 addresses and their counts
  witness 2026-10-04 the table showed what the owner's JSON did not make visible: 368 addresses the owner reports as conflicts with a content change where neither observation retained anything -- two 403 refusals whose error pages differ by a few bytes (https://aaa.si.edu/ is one). The owner counts a change of digest between any two observations, not between two that held content
  forbidden_inference a conflict reported by the owner -> content that changed; 368 of 12,991 on 2026-10-04 were refusals whose refusal page changed
  known_gap the owner's conflict rule has not been changed, so those 368 are still reported; whether a changed refusal page is a conflict is the owner's question to settle
  witness 2026-10-04 the live retained bodies compressed in place by the filesystem, each copy compared to its original before replacing it: 3,074 files, 897 MB on disk to 483 MB, with every reader unchanged because they still open ordinary files
  known_gap bodies are held by filesystem compression, not by the body pack: the pack would take them to 391 MB but nothing reads bodies from it. A file rewritten later is written uncompressed until the pass is run again, and the compression is this filesystem's, not a portable form. A body of 256 MB or more is left out of a pack and counted
  known_gap packing is whole-directory and from scratch; there is no incremental append, and a build without zstd neither writes nor reads a pack and says so

interaction receipt-pack-serves-foreign-twin
  source receipt-pack
  destination foreign-twin
  relation stores_for
  preserves every receipt whole and byte-identical under its file name, and the rule that the newest word on a receipt is the loose file
  drops the one-file-per-receipt layout and its filesystem block per receipt
  forbidden_inference an answer read from the pack -> a different answer than the files would give; the witness holds them identical
  invalidates_on the receipt schema, the loader's eight-kilobyte reading window, or the pack's table layout changes
  witness components/BonfyreProject/tests/receipt_pack_test.sh
  binding components/BonfyreProject/src/main.c

architecture media-decode-operation
  canonical_name MediaDecodeOperation
  family external
  domain External / One media operation, several engines
  layer external
  semantic_question Can "decode this picture to pixels" be one operation with a browser engine and a native engine behind it, held to the same answer?
  purpose The decode operation as a shared unit: the same source, the same receipt (frame count, size, pixel layout, digest of the pixels), realised by the browser's WebCodecs decoder and by FFmpeg's native libraries. A consumer names the operation and takes whichever engine is where the work is.
  maturity measured
  node_types DecodeReceipt
  interacts browser-engine-provider
  source_path integrations/browser/engines/media-decode.mjs
  source_path integrations/browser/tests/media-decode.test.mjs
  witness integrations/browser/tests/media-decode.test.mjs decodes a 320 by 240 PNG through both engines to the same pixel digest, an AV1 still to pictures within four levels per channel of each other, and tells a different picture apart
  witness 2026-10-04 Chrome 146 WebCodecs against FFmpeg with dav1d: the PNG identical in every channel; the AV1 still differing by at most 2 levels, mean 0.28, because the conversion from the codec's colour space to RGB is not defined bit for bit
  invariant a lossless source must decode to identical pixels in both engines; a lossy one is compared within a stated tolerance, never by digest
  forbidden_inference the same pixel digest for a lossless picture -> the same for a lossy one
  forbidden_inference one still picture decoded -> video, audio, timed frames or a live capture decoded; only the first frame of a still is realised
  known_gap one operation and one frame: no audio, no video stream, no time base beyond the frame's own timestamp, and no encode. Nothing in the estate calls it

architecture text-primitives
  canonical_name TextPrimitives
  family external
  domain External / Text identity and bounded matching
  layer external
  semantic_question Is this the same name as that one, and will this pattern finish?
  purpose Two primitives over packaged libraries, for every owner that compares names or runs rules: an identity key (utf8proc: composed, case-folded, compatibility forms unified, invisible code points dropped, spacing collapsed) and a matcher guaranteed linear in the text (RE2), which refuses a pattern that needs backtracking.
  maturity measured
  node_types IdentityKey
  node_types MatchProgram
  source_path lib/libbonfyre-text/bf_text.h
  source_path lib/libbonfyre-text/bf_text.cc
  source_path lib/libbonfyre-text/tests/bf_text_test.c
  source_path lib/libbonfyre-text/bf_text_key.c
  witness lib/libbonfyre-text/tests/bf_text_test.c, from C: composed and decomposed accents, case, the sharp s, fullwidth letters, a ligature, a no-break space and a zero-width space all give one key; an accent, and a space between words, keep two names apart; invalid UTF-8 and a key that does not fit are refused; a backreference and a lookahead are refused with a reason; and ^(a+)+$ over 200,001 characters answers in under a millisecond
  witness 2026-10-04 the identity key over the live estate's 29,240 distinct source names gave 29,238 keys; the two collisions differ only by case. No Unicode identity split exists among those names today
  forbidden_inference the same identity key -> the same person or organisation; it is the same spelling
  known_gap no owner calls either primitive yet: CMS, Entity, ForeignTwin and the frontier still compare text their own way, and their rules still run on each language's own regular-expression engine

architecture estate-volume-harvest
  canonical_name EstateVolumeHarvest
  family external
  domain External / What is still only on the old host's disk
  layer external
  semantic_question A 47 GB boot volume is being held because the real auth store and CMS are on it. How little of it is actually the estate's, and how does that come home?
  purpose Reads a mounted volume read-only and takes out only the estate's own state: inventoried, each copy checked against the volume, each database integrity-checked, keys and credential files named and not copied, and the rest packed into one ordered archive with a receipt. The volume stops being the only copy of anything without being written to or deleted.
  maturity implemented
  node_types HarvestManifest
  node_types HarvestReceipt
  source_path scripts/oracle_volume_harvest.sh
  source_path scripts/tests/oracle_volume_harvest_test.sh
  source_path scripts/claim_oracle_a1.sh
  witness scripts/tests/oracle_volume_harvest_test.sh runs the harvest over a made-up read-only volume: 29 estate files taken and none from the operating system or caches, two key and credential files named and not copied, a damaged database named as failing integrity, the auth store readable at the destination, the archive holding every taken file, the volume byte-identical afterwards, and a harvest onto the volume itself refused
  witness 2026-10-04 read-only inventory of the tenancy: bonfyre-runtime-01 (Boot Volume) 47 GB and a rescue clone bonfyre-estate-rescue-2026-09-18 47 GB, both in AD-1; one full backup bonfyre-runtime-01-state-2026-10-03 (47 GB, 37 GB unique); one bucket; no instance. The claim job had asked for a host 1,026 times, every answer "Out of host capacity"
  invariant the harvest never writes to the volume, never deletes, and never copies a private key or credential file
  forbidden_inference the harvest tested on a made-up volume -> the real volume harvested; it has never been mounted, because no host exists to attach it to
  forbidden_inference a harvested file -> a current one; the volume stopped changing when its instance was disabled and the estate at home has moved on
  known_gap nothing can read the real volume until the claim job wins a host. The paths the harvest looks under are taken from the old host's layout as recorded, not from the volume
  known_gap reconciling what comes home with the local estate (which auth rows and CMS entries exist only there) is not written, and neither volume nor the backup is released by anything here
